00:01:59.630 --> 00:02:00.600
A few hands.

00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:03.230
OK, that's actually
kind of awesome.

00:02:03.230 --> 00:02:06.710
Our story is a little different
than the stereotypical Silicon

00:02:06.710 --> 00:02:08.990
Valley startup.

00:02:08.990 --> 00:02:11.870
While we do have Silicon
Valley VC funding

00:02:11.870 --> 00:02:16.340
and we do have veteran
Silicon Valley leadership,

00:02:16.340 --> 00:02:17.810
we're actually quite--

00:02:17.810 --> 00:02:20.840
and we have offices
here and in New York--

00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:22.400
our headquarters
are actually way up

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:24.470
in the mountains of
Santa Fe, New Mexico.

00:02:24.470 --> 00:02:28.220
And it's kind of a weird place
for a startup in one way,

00:02:28.220 --> 00:02:31.430
but that's because myself and
the rest of the founding team

00:02:31.430 --> 00:02:36.500
were scientists, researchers,
and engineers at Los Alamos

00:02:36.500 --> 00:02:38.060
National Laboratory.

00:02:38.060 --> 00:02:40.100
And there, we were
actually working on machine

00:02:40.100 --> 00:02:44.390
learning and applying that to
a range of real world problems,

00:02:44.390 --> 00:02:47.450
including remotely
sensing the Earth.

00:02:47.450 --> 00:02:50.870
And so the Cloud, we
don't have a data center.

00:02:50.870 --> 00:02:54.030
We don't even own
a single server.

00:02:54.030 --> 00:02:56.240
So the Cloud, we
basically couldn't exist

00:02:56.240 --> 00:02:58.170
without this infrastructure.

00:02:58.170 --> 00:03:03.110
Yet, on any given day, our staff
of scientists can build models,

00:03:03.110 --> 00:03:05.990
and they can build them
over petabytes and petabytes

00:03:05.990 --> 00:03:06.920
of data.

00:03:06.920 --> 00:03:09.050
And they can scale them
on thousands of cores.

00:03:09.050 --> 00:03:11.280
And that's all basically
because of the Cloud,

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:14.150
and that that's
really why we're here.

00:03:14.150 --> 00:03:17.570
So our story is a bit of--

00:03:17.570 --> 00:03:20.300
in some ways, it's
really about sensors.

00:03:20.300 --> 00:03:25.340
And in some ways we're
looking at sensing the Earth.

00:03:25.340 --> 00:03:29.530
But sensors are really at an
inflection point right now.

00:03:29.530 --> 00:03:32.030
They're changing
from a world where

00:03:32.030 --> 00:03:35.270
data was kind of individually
collected or manually

00:03:35.270 --> 00:03:38.480
entered to one
where it's basically

00:03:38.480 --> 00:03:43.140
being continuously sourced and
automatically input by sensors.

00:03:43.140 --> 00:03:46.790
They're getting better
and better resolution.

00:03:46.790 --> 00:03:49.820
We're seeing better range,
better data transmissions

00:03:49.820 --> 00:03:53.690
of sensors, and the costs are
just dramatically decreasing.

00:03:53.690 --> 00:03:56.420
This is everything from things
I use riding my mountain

00:03:56.420 --> 00:03:59.780
bike but all the way
up into the satellites

00:03:59.780 --> 00:04:02.570
and remotely sensing the Earth.

00:04:02.570 --> 00:04:06.590
Just not that long ago,
almost all satellites

00:04:06.590 --> 00:04:08.930
were basically
government funded.

00:04:08.930 --> 00:04:11.180
So they cost
hundreds of millions

00:04:11.180 --> 00:04:12.650
of dollars for a project.

00:04:12.650 --> 00:04:16.820
And now, an engineering lab
student can make a CubeSat

00:04:16.820 --> 00:04:18.890
and can launch
that up into space.

00:04:18.890 --> 00:04:21.920
Or they can mount a sensor that
they've made in a laboratory

00:04:21.920 --> 00:04:24.260
to measure things
like radio frequency.

00:04:24.260 --> 00:04:27.260
They can mount that to a drone
or fly it around on a plane.

00:04:27.260 --> 00:04:30.140
So this world is changing,
and it's basically

00:04:30.140 --> 00:04:33.110
becoming a continuous--

00:04:33.110 --> 00:04:37.040
we've gone from these infrequent
samples to almost continuous--

00:04:37.040 --> 00:04:40.210
excuse me-- continuous
measurements.

00:04:40.210 --> 00:04:43.050
And one of the really
big core challenges

00:04:43.050 --> 00:04:45.390
that we've always
had with satellites

00:04:45.390 --> 00:04:49.740
and remote sensing of data is
getting access to the data.

00:04:49.740 --> 00:04:52.500
Sure, you could click
and download an image,

00:04:52.500 --> 00:04:55.840
and it was made for
that human interaction

00:04:55.840 --> 00:04:57.420
and that human analyst.

00:04:57.420 --> 00:05:00.450
But with machine learning,
that really has changed.

00:05:00.450 --> 00:05:03.210
We can process and
look at imagery

00:05:03.210 --> 00:05:07.320
that humans will never, ever
look at in our lifetimes

00:05:07.320 --> 00:05:10.200
again, because there's
just so much data.

00:05:10.200 --> 00:05:12.900
And the Cloud has been
one of the big enablers

00:05:12.900 --> 00:05:13.845
in the last few years.

00:05:17.130 --> 00:05:19.200
It's now finally
possible to process

00:05:19.200 --> 00:05:23.340
and have every single
piece of satellite imagery

00:05:23.340 --> 00:05:26.760
or aerial imagery or
remotely sensing data

00:05:26.760 --> 00:05:29.850
and have that available and
basically build that and make

00:05:29.850 --> 00:05:31.950
it machine learning-ready.

00:05:31.950 --> 00:05:36.690
And so this imagery that
we put into our platform

00:05:36.690 --> 00:05:39.770
is fully global in coverage.

00:05:39.770 --> 00:05:43.820
And then, it crosses many
different spectrums of light.

00:05:43.820 --> 00:05:49.350
Behind me, that's showing
near-IR on a global composite.

00:05:49.350 --> 00:05:53.840
And then, it's across
many different modalities.

00:05:53.840 --> 00:05:55.520
Sure, we have human
visible light,

00:05:55.520 --> 00:05:59.690
but this is something called
synthetic aperture radar.

00:05:59.690 --> 00:06:02.150
It's not where the sun
shines off the Earth,

00:06:02.150 --> 00:06:05.000
bounces off the Earth,
and a camera captures.

00:06:05.000 --> 00:06:07.700
Instead, the satellite emits
a radar signal actually

00:06:07.700 --> 00:06:10.910
down onto the Earth, bounces
that off, and captures it.

00:06:10.910 --> 00:06:12.950
It's giving us a global
height measurement

00:06:12.950 --> 00:06:15.180
around the whole world.

00:06:15.180 --> 00:06:16.790
And then, it's through time.

00:06:16.790 --> 00:06:19.550
And often, it's this
temporal cadence

00:06:19.550 --> 00:06:23.270
that provides maybe the
greatest analytical insight

00:06:23.270 --> 00:06:25.100
when we're building models.

00:06:25.100 --> 00:06:27.824
Behind me is NASA's
MODIS imagery.

00:06:27.824 --> 00:06:29.240
This is showing
vegetative health.

00:06:29.240 --> 00:06:31.010
That's that really,
really bright green.

00:06:31.010 --> 00:06:34.350
And this is showing a
single frame for every day.

00:06:34.350 --> 00:06:37.180
So this is looking down
everywhere in the US every day,

00:06:37.180 --> 00:06:40.580
and this is 15 years of imagery
that's playing behind you.

00:06:40.580 --> 00:06:44.390
So you're seeing a half billion
pixels in every single frame

00:06:44.390 --> 00:06:46.480
of that video.

00:06:46.480 --> 00:06:50.710
And so visibility and
accuracy advantage

00:06:50.710 --> 00:06:55.280
comes from the ability
to combine multiple input

00:06:55.280 --> 00:06:57.020
sources into the models.

00:06:57.020 --> 00:07:00.040
There's not really one
data set that provides you

00:07:00.040 --> 00:07:03.730
all the answers to the really
consequential problems.

00:07:03.730 --> 00:07:08.620
And so these are across
spatial, spectral, and temporal.

00:07:08.620 --> 00:07:10.935
And it's this fusion
of both internal data

00:07:10.935 --> 00:07:16.010
sets that we build as a company,
but it's also public data sets.

00:07:16.010 --> 00:07:17.680
We have a lot of stuff--

00:07:17.680 --> 00:07:21.190
imagery from, say, NASA
or European Space Agency,

00:07:21.190 --> 00:07:22.870
institutions like that.

00:07:22.870 --> 00:07:25.030
It's commercial data
sets, and they all

00:07:25.030 --> 00:07:30.400
have a critical time and a place
context that's really required

00:07:30.400 --> 00:07:34.900
to produce accurate models.

00:07:34.900 --> 00:07:37.720
So right here, I would
like to just dive

00:07:37.720 --> 00:07:40.140
deeper into what
I'm talking about.

00:07:40.140 --> 00:07:44.800
As an example, this
is Des Moines, Iowa.

00:07:44.800 --> 00:07:45.910
It's kind of a--

00:07:45.910 --> 00:07:48.970
I don't know-- do we have
anyone from Des Moines?

00:07:48.970 --> 00:07:49.870
I don't see anyone.

00:07:49.870 --> 00:07:50.590
OK.

00:07:50.590 --> 00:07:53.200
I didn't think so,
but you never know.

00:07:53.200 --> 00:07:57.340
So this is a combination
of urban and agricultural

00:07:57.340 --> 00:07:58.510
landscapes.

00:07:58.510 --> 00:08:01.090
We're seeing the vector
data on the right.

00:08:01.090 --> 00:08:03.130
This could be
GroundTruth locations,

00:08:03.130 --> 00:08:05.650
or maybe it's one of
our customer's pieces

00:08:05.650 --> 00:08:07.480
of infrastructure
that they've wanted

00:08:07.480 --> 00:08:10.880
to tag for changes over time.

00:08:10.880 --> 00:08:13.490
And then we have--
on the far left,

00:08:13.490 --> 00:08:16.390
we can show vegetation
and vegetative health.

00:08:16.390 --> 00:08:19.240
And in the middle, you can see
that synthetic aperture radar

00:08:19.240 --> 00:08:20.770
I was talking about earlier.

00:08:20.770 --> 00:08:25.840
The really bright pink and
white is higher-height objects.

00:08:25.840 --> 00:08:29.740
And the challenge is making
a range of different sensor

00:08:29.740 --> 00:08:33.909
outputs from a range of
different agencies like NASA

00:08:33.909 --> 00:08:35.799
or European Space Agency.

00:08:35.799 --> 00:08:38.289
Make it all uniform
and queryable,

00:08:38.289 --> 00:08:40.480
so they can be used as all--

00:08:40.480 --> 00:08:42.039
all as inputs for those models.

00:08:44.610 --> 00:08:49.700
And so our customers and
our team of applied research

00:08:49.700 --> 00:08:56.150
scientists solved a pretty
broad range of problems with ML.

00:08:56.150 --> 00:09:02.825
And so every week, we bring
new sensors and new data

00:09:02.825 --> 00:09:06.060
sets in to try and
solve those problems.

00:09:06.060 --> 00:09:09.230
So what are some of
those examples of types

00:09:09.230 --> 00:09:10.390
of models that we've built?

00:09:12.980 --> 00:09:18.140
To extract the most data, we've
built this modeling platform

00:09:18.140 --> 00:09:20.030
for complex global systems.

00:09:20.030 --> 00:09:22.580
And now, we have
around 15 petabytes

00:09:22.580 --> 00:09:24.920
of data in our archive.

00:09:24.920 --> 00:09:29.960
And with this full historical
time stack, what it does

00:09:29.960 --> 00:09:33.050
is allow us to go
back through time

00:09:33.050 --> 00:09:35.950
and track both
conditions and outcomes,

00:09:35.950 --> 00:09:38.720
so we can generalize
that relationship.

00:09:38.720 --> 00:09:41.450
And then, we can use
it to make predictions

00:09:41.450 --> 00:09:44.840
based on today's observations.

00:09:44.840 --> 00:09:46.730
And then, we can
track new outcomes,

00:09:46.730 --> 00:09:49.200
and we can measure accuracy.

00:09:49.200 --> 00:09:51.290
And we can basically
continuously

00:09:51.290 --> 00:09:52.660
be updating these models.

00:09:52.660 --> 00:09:53.900
So this is an example.

00:09:53.900 --> 00:09:58.220
This is showing construction
and buildings being started,

00:09:58.220 --> 00:10:02.460
and that's correlated
to steel demand.

00:10:02.460 --> 00:10:08.160
This is another example
showing object detection.

00:10:08.160 --> 00:10:11.250
We are doing detection
on buildings,

00:10:11.250 --> 00:10:14.880
and we have run this on the
entire state of California,

00:10:14.880 --> 00:10:18.132
as well as numerous
other parts of the world.

00:10:18.132 --> 00:10:19.590
And actually, one
of our scientists

00:10:19.590 --> 00:10:23.280
was just telling me yesterday
that last Friday, we

00:10:23.280 --> 00:10:25.710
were trying to understand
things like hurricanes

00:10:25.710 --> 00:10:28.800
and maybe how hurricanes might
impact a state like Florida.

00:10:28.800 --> 00:10:31.230
And in just a few hours,
they were able to scale up

00:10:31.230 --> 00:10:33.290
and basically map out
every single building

00:10:33.290 --> 00:10:34.290
in the state of Florida.

00:10:37.840 --> 00:10:41.410
Another example is more
from the agriculture side,

00:10:41.410 --> 00:10:44.170
and that is crop classification.

00:10:44.170 --> 00:10:46.840
In the center image,
you see that--

00:10:46.840 --> 00:10:48.400
all those really bright colors.

00:10:48.400 --> 00:10:51.430
What we've done is
actually segment

00:10:51.430 --> 00:10:54.310
out different
agricultural fields

00:10:54.310 --> 00:10:56.590
to be able to identify
which crops are growing.

00:10:56.590 --> 00:11:00.250
Is there wheat or soybeans
or barley or something else?

00:11:00.250 --> 00:11:04.210
And then, we can use that
historical set of data

00:11:04.210 --> 00:11:06.280
to go back and
train through time

00:11:06.280 --> 00:11:09.940
and see how those models
performed in various seasons.

00:11:09.940 --> 00:11:11.980
Some years, you
might have a drought.

00:11:11.980 --> 00:11:15.105
Other years, we have tons
of rain and flooding.

00:11:15.105 --> 00:11:16.480
So you need to be
able to compare

00:11:16.480 --> 00:11:20.940
with known truths over time.

00:11:20.940 --> 00:11:22.950
And to give you just an idea--

00:11:22.950 --> 00:11:24.330
this is a tech conference.

00:11:24.330 --> 00:11:26.910
And we talk a lot
about compute and scale

00:11:26.910 --> 00:11:28.350
and things like that--

00:11:28.350 --> 00:11:32.490
just to quantify what type
of scale some of these models

00:11:32.490 --> 00:11:33.630
run at.

00:11:33.630 --> 00:11:36.540
In 2015, we were just a
few-month-old company,

00:11:36.540 --> 00:11:40.110
and we made our first
agricultural models.

00:11:40.110 --> 00:11:43.350
And that was looking at
the entire United States

00:11:43.350 --> 00:11:47.250
and our production of corn.

00:11:47.250 --> 00:11:50.520
And so to build those
models, we processed

00:11:50.520 --> 00:11:53.680
around 4 quadrillion
pixels of imagery,

00:11:53.680 --> 00:11:58.530
so that's hundreds of
billions of iPhone photos.

00:11:58.530 --> 00:12:00.540
And we wanted to
build a platform

00:12:00.540 --> 00:12:04.710
that we really optimized
more for data scientist time.

00:12:04.710 --> 00:12:08.190
We wanted to promote rapid
iterations of models.

00:12:08.190 --> 00:12:12.190
To get the accuracy we were
looking for on that example,

00:12:12.190 --> 00:12:16.080
it took nearly 1,000
iterations in just a few weeks.

00:12:16.080 --> 00:12:18.720
So you can't get
that rapid cadence

00:12:18.720 --> 00:12:20.910
of iterations on your
models without being

00:12:20.910 --> 00:12:23.910
able to scale horizontally.

00:12:23.910 --> 00:12:26.520
And so our team uses
our platform every day

00:12:26.520 --> 00:12:28.740
to continuously
improve those products

00:12:28.740 --> 00:12:31.870
and improve the scaling.

00:12:31.870 --> 00:12:33.580
So hopefully, that
provides you an idea

00:12:33.580 --> 00:12:37.510
with a breadth of the
types of ML models

00:12:37.510 --> 00:12:39.400
that we wanted to
be able to run.

00:12:39.400 --> 00:12:43.120
That was kind of the goal of
what we were trying to build.

00:12:43.120 --> 00:12:44.800
What does our
workflow look like?

00:12:44.800 --> 00:12:46.690
Well, we start with
the discovery phase.

00:12:46.690 --> 00:12:48.700
We investigate a hypothesis.

00:12:48.700 --> 00:12:51.190
We look at what types of
data sets we have available,

00:12:51.190 --> 00:12:54.490
maybe what other data set we
could bring in to help fuse

00:12:54.490 --> 00:12:56.710
together and provide insight.

00:12:56.710 --> 00:12:58.630
And then, we build a
lot of candidate models

00:12:58.630 --> 00:13:00.730
off those data sets.

00:13:00.730 --> 00:13:03.730
We create and evaluate
those over historical

00:13:03.730 --> 00:13:04.930
or maybe a known truth.

00:13:04.930 --> 00:13:08.920
Maybe I knew where a
location of a building

00:13:08.920 --> 00:13:12.340
was or a radio tower
or something like that.

00:13:12.340 --> 00:13:17.320
And then, that model that starts
being evaluated and tested.

00:13:17.320 --> 00:13:19.340
And finally, it goes
into production.

00:13:19.340 --> 00:13:22.330
And then, as we get new sensor
data in all the time just

00:13:22.330 --> 00:13:24.650
streaming, it's
continuously updated,

00:13:24.650 --> 00:13:26.950
and it basically generates
a continuous time

00:13:26.950 --> 00:13:29.790
series of analytics.

00:13:29.790 --> 00:13:33.540
So you can use our tools
to make your own models,

00:13:33.540 --> 00:13:36.600
or our team of data
scientists can actually

00:13:36.600 --> 00:13:38.950
build them on your behalf.

00:13:38.950 --> 00:13:40.450
And so at this
point, I'd like to do

00:13:40.450 --> 00:13:42.640
a really quick demonstration.

00:13:47.840 --> 00:13:50.225
And it's a demonstration
of typing my password.

00:13:56.864 --> 00:13:58.530
What we did was we
took a big composite.

00:13:58.530 --> 00:14:01.290
This is aerial imagery
of the entire US.

00:14:01.290 --> 00:14:02.580
This is all actually stored.

00:14:02.580 --> 00:14:08.040
We don't actually store
any of this on PD disks.

00:14:08.040 --> 00:14:09.300
Oh, haven't switched yet.

00:14:09.300 --> 00:14:10.785
SPEAKER 1: [INAUDIBLE].

00:14:31.575 --> 00:14:33.060
OK, [INAUDIBLE].

00:14:38.505 --> 00:14:40.980
Are you good?

00:14:40.980 --> 00:14:42.260
TIM KELTON: Oh, here we go.

00:14:42.260 --> 00:14:45.500
All right, try two.

00:14:45.500 --> 00:14:47.540
So one interesting thing--

00:14:47.540 --> 00:14:50.030
none of this data is
actually stored on PD disks.

00:14:50.030 --> 00:14:52.850
This is all being
served straight off GCS.

00:14:52.850 --> 00:14:56.210
So that kind of gives you an
idea of the speed of the Google

00:14:56.210 --> 00:14:58.640
network and GCS storage.

00:14:58.640 --> 00:15:00.650
But this is a huge map.

00:15:00.650 --> 00:15:03.390
This is tens of
terabytes of imagery.

00:15:03.390 --> 00:15:07.220
So if you had to zoom in
and look for objects inside

00:15:07.220 --> 00:15:09.980
of that, say,
football fields, and I

00:15:09.980 --> 00:15:12.800
needed to look across terabytes
and terabytes of imagery

00:15:12.800 --> 00:15:16.520
and find every single
football field, that would

00:15:16.520 --> 00:15:18.840
take a huge amount of time.

00:15:18.840 --> 00:15:23.090
And so what we've done is we've
gridded that huge composite

00:15:23.090 --> 00:15:26.810
image into tiny little grids,
and then we've built a feature

00:15:26.810 --> 00:15:31.160
vector of each of those
tiny little 128-by-128-pixel

00:15:31.160 --> 00:15:31.820
vectors.

00:15:31.820 --> 00:15:34.340
And then, we have similarity
search-ranked them.

00:15:34.340 --> 00:15:37.400
And so what you're seeing is the
most similar objects to the one

00:15:37.400 --> 00:15:38.630
I just selected.

00:15:38.630 --> 00:15:40.370
And so I could do
this for anything.

00:15:40.370 --> 00:15:43.760
It could even be the railroad
tracks right next to it.

00:15:43.760 --> 00:15:46.490
And it shows me all of them all
of the most similar objects all

00:15:46.490 --> 00:15:48.560
across that entire composite.

00:15:48.560 --> 00:15:54.860
So that's a really quick
example of the types of models

00:15:54.860 --> 00:15:57.290
we're trying to build.

00:15:57.290 --> 00:15:59.540
So at this point, just
talking a little bit more

00:15:59.540 --> 00:16:03.410
about the GCP
infrastructure that we use--

00:16:03.410 --> 00:16:09.020
we kind of have two main
portions of our infrastructure

00:16:09.020 --> 00:16:10.310
that we've--

00:16:10.310 --> 00:16:11.480
we have the ingestion.

00:16:11.480 --> 00:16:13.940
And that's a very
large bulk processing.

00:16:13.940 --> 00:16:17.600
And getting all of that
data machine learning ready

00:16:17.600 --> 00:16:19.340
is kind of how we phrase it.

00:16:19.340 --> 00:16:21.590
And then there's our
core APIs, and those

00:16:21.590 --> 00:16:25.130
are more microservices-based.

00:16:25.130 --> 00:16:28.770
And that's where we run all
of our user workloads as well.

00:16:28.770 --> 00:16:33.050
So let's dive in to a
little bit what we do.

00:16:33.050 --> 00:16:35.840
To get that imagery
ready, that's

00:16:35.840 --> 00:16:38.360
actually one of the
biggest computational jobs

00:16:38.360 --> 00:16:40.580
we run as a company.

00:16:40.580 --> 00:16:44.210
We have to convert and
georegister all of our imagery

00:16:44.210 --> 00:16:45.930
for accurate alignment.

00:16:45.930 --> 00:16:49.430
We have to convert it
to standard projections.

00:16:49.430 --> 00:16:53.040
We need to do things like
remove clouds and cloud shadows.

00:16:53.040 --> 00:16:54.620
These are really
pesky problems that

00:16:54.620 --> 00:16:56.310
are kind of hard to deal with.

00:16:56.310 --> 00:16:59.930
We have to adjust for the camera
angle of the sensor, the sun's

00:16:59.930 --> 00:17:03.890
angle-- how that might affect
what we're seeing back.

00:17:03.890 --> 00:17:06.470
We have to calibrate the
sensors against themselves

00:17:06.470 --> 00:17:09.770
and their neighbors, and
compensate for degradations

00:17:09.770 --> 00:17:11.450
at the top of the atmosphere.

00:17:11.450 --> 00:17:15.950
So our goal is to take 20 years
of camera technology, which

00:17:15.950 --> 00:17:19.040
things have changed
a bit in 20 years,

00:17:19.040 --> 00:17:22.500
and to make those sensor
outputs uniform and queryable

00:17:22.500 --> 00:17:26.079
and searchable all on the fly.

00:17:26.079 --> 00:17:29.650
This is a real example
of what that looks like.

00:17:29.650 --> 00:17:31.820
On the left, you
see raw completely

00:17:31.820 --> 00:17:35.040
uncalibrated,
uncorrected imagery.

00:17:35.040 --> 00:17:38.730
Notice the calibration issues,
the cloud cover, all of that.

00:17:38.730 --> 00:17:43.120
Then as you move right, you
can see the corrected versions.

00:17:43.120 --> 00:17:44.920
We also store vector data.

00:17:44.920 --> 00:17:48.240
An example of vector data would
be maybe outlines of counties.

00:17:48.240 --> 00:17:52.600
It could be state outlines,
city outlines, things like that.

00:17:52.600 --> 00:17:55.740
Another example
might be statistics.

00:17:55.740 --> 00:17:58.560
In this example, it's
actually the Panama Canal,

00:17:58.560 --> 00:18:00.940
if you didn't recognize that.

00:18:00.940 --> 00:18:03.840
And one thing you
might have is some sort

00:18:03.840 --> 00:18:05.650
of a statistical data set.

00:18:05.650 --> 00:18:08.820
You might say how many ships
pass through this portion

00:18:08.820 --> 00:18:11.130
of the image.

00:18:11.130 --> 00:18:13.760
So this is, from an
architectural perspective,

00:18:13.760 --> 00:18:19.760
what those large bulk processing
workloads look like on GCP.

00:18:19.760 --> 00:18:23.030
We source that data
and bring that in.

00:18:23.030 --> 00:18:27.680
And then, we use Pub/Sub
for breaking all of our work

00:18:27.680 --> 00:18:32.240
up into distributed
asynchronous tasks.

00:18:32.240 --> 00:18:35.450
And Pub/Sub, we've found,
has scaled tremendously.

00:18:35.450 --> 00:18:37.890
We can really, really
hit it quite hard.

00:18:37.890 --> 00:18:40.100
And these asynchronous
tasks actually

00:18:40.100 --> 00:18:44.030
fit really nicely into
things like preemptible VMs,

00:18:44.030 --> 00:18:48.480
because each piece of work
is very, very small in unit.

00:18:48.480 --> 00:18:50.420
And those machines
can go away tomorrow,

00:18:50.420 --> 00:18:52.980
and we'll just keep
spinning up new ones.

00:18:52.980 --> 00:18:54.770
So we love the cost
savings that we

00:18:54.770 --> 00:18:56.930
can get when we leverage that.

00:18:56.930 --> 00:19:00.470
And one of the first
examples of how we actually

00:19:00.470 --> 00:19:02.590
gained a lot of
confidence that we would

00:19:02.590 --> 00:19:05.840
be able to do this type
of workload on the Cloud

00:19:05.840 --> 00:19:09.220
was scaling up and processing
a petabyte of imagery.

00:19:09.220 --> 00:19:12.680
And we were able to scale that
up to around a 30,000 CPU core

00:19:12.680 --> 00:19:16.600
job and then run and process
a petabyte of imagery

00:19:16.600 --> 00:19:18.290
in about 16 hours.

00:19:18.290 --> 00:19:20.690
And that gave us
confidence not just

00:19:20.690 --> 00:19:22.910
that we could get tons
of cores, because we know

00:19:22.910 --> 00:19:24.710
we can do that almost anywhere.

00:19:24.710 --> 00:19:26.660
The bigger and harder
problems actually

00:19:26.660 --> 00:19:28.580
are with the object storage.

00:19:28.580 --> 00:19:31.040
Would GCS be able
to scale with that?

00:19:31.040 --> 00:19:32.690
Would the actual
network, as we kept

00:19:32.690 --> 00:19:36.740
adding more and more nodes,
would that be able to scale?

00:19:36.740 --> 00:19:39.500
And so we've actually
seen close to

00:19:39.500 --> 00:19:43.180
high-performance supercomputing
speeds on the GCS networks.

00:19:47.500 --> 00:19:50.170
And then, we have
these other data sets.

00:19:50.170 --> 00:19:52.330
They could be metadata
or information

00:19:52.330 --> 00:19:57.100
about those raw pixels or
what we refer to as rasters.

00:19:57.100 --> 00:19:58.810
That could be
informations about what

00:19:58.810 --> 00:20:02.740
bands of light, what modalities,
what periods in time, things

00:20:02.740 --> 00:20:03.900
like that.

00:20:03.900 --> 00:20:07.270
We use all types of different
persistent databases

00:20:07.270 --> 00:20:08.710
to store that.

00:20:08.710 --> 00:20:12.880
And then for error
logging, tracing,

00:20:12.880 --> 00:20:15.120
things like that, we're not
really in that business.

00:20:15.120 --> 00:20:17.840
So we're happy to try
and use a no-op solution.

00:20:17.840 --> 00:20:20.230
So there, we used things
like Stackdriver's tracing

00:20:20.230 --> 00:20:22.790
error and monitoring.

00:20:22.790 --> 00:20:25.720
So in terms of
our core APIs, how

00:20:25.720 --> 00:20:29.830
do those microservices look?

00:20:29.830 --> 00:20:33.460
We have around approximately
50 microservices.

00:20:33.460 --> 00:20:37.060
Any given day, we have a new
pre-alpha or alpha service

00:20:37.060 --> 00:20:37.660
coming up.

00:20:37.660 --> 00:20:39.770
We have canaries
that we're trying out

00:20:39.770 --> 00:20:40.730
and things like that.

00:20:40.730 --> 00:20:43.280
So it's always in flux.

00:20:43.280 --> 00:20:47.870
But one of the core things, the
reason we have microservices

00:20:47.870 --> 00:20:51.250
and we adapt them, is we have
so many different data types.

00:20:51.250 --> 00:20:54.660
It's actually really
handy to be able to pair

00:20:54.660 --> 00:20:57.310
the persistent
backend storage with--

00:20:57.310 --> 00:20:59.650
for each microservice
uniquely and then

00:20:59.650 --> 00:21:02.110
be able to scale
them individually.

00:21:02.110 --> 00:21:05.680
And Kubernetes
multi-tenancy policies

00:21:05.680 --> 00:21:09.910
are generally set, most of the
time, on a namespace basis.

00:21:09.910 --> 00:21:15.950
And we isolate various
microservices by namespace,

00:21:15.950 --> 00:21:18.610
so then, we can explicitly
define policies.

00:21:18.610 --> 00:21:21.460
We can explicitly
define how exactly

00:21:21.460 --> 00:21:25.390
we want to see it scale
and react to load.

00:21:25.390 --> 00:21:27.490
We can do scaling interaction.

00:21:27.490 --> 00:21:29.230
We can control the
interaction that

00:21:29.230 --> 00:21:32.170
goes through the control plane.

00:21:32.170 --> 00:21:36.430
So that actually goes
through our core APIs,

00:21:36.430 --> 00:21:40.840
and those core APIs become kind
of the heartbeat of our system.

00:21:40.840 --> 00:21:44.110
Like we really need to know
and get as much visibility

00:21:44.110 --> 00:21:45.070
into that.

00:21:45.070 --> 00:21:47.410
Our end users, those
workloads are also

00:21:47.410 --> 00:21:49.930
broke out into their
own namespaces.

00:21:49.930 --> 00:21:52.120
Those workloads don't
have any interaction

00:21:52.120 --> 00:21:54.580
with the backend
Kubernetes control plane.

00:21:54.580 --> 00:21:56.110
For the most part,
that's all going

00:21:56.110 --> 00:21:58.780
through our centralized
API, and that's

00:21:58.780 --> 00:22:02.290
because on any given user, they
might be able to scale jobs

00:22:02.290 --> 00:22:04.380
to 10,000 cores.

00:22:04.380 --> 00:22:06.610
And that's not that
uncommon for us,

00:22:06.610 --> 00:22:12.430
so we need to really funnel that
information almost as a proxy.

00:22:12.430 --> 00:22:17.350
So here's how at least I have
thought about multi-tenancy

00:22:17.350 --> 00:22:18.340
on Kubernetes.

00:22:18.340 --> 00:22:19.960
This is an extremely
broad subject.

00:22:19.960 --> 00:22:22.120
We could have a three-day
conference on it.

00:22:22.120 --> 00:22:25.120
And there's an amazing
Kubernetes SIG working group

00:22:25.120 --> 00:22:28.150
that I'd highly recommend if you
really want to dive super deep.

00:22:28.150 --> 00:22:30.350
They have calls every week.

00:22:30.350 --> 00:22:32.200
But for this, let's
define multi-tenancy

00:22:32.200 --> 00:22:34.720
as a way to provide
isolation for each tenant.

00:22:34.720 --> 00:22:37.200
That could be a user
or an application.

00:22:37.200 --> 00:22:38.640
And on one extreme--

00:22:38.640 --> 00:22:40.180
that would be the far left--

00:22:40.180 --> 00:22:44.200
all of the users are
actually in an enterprise.

00:22:44.200 --> 00:22:46.210
I know we don't trust
all our coworkers,

00:22:46.210 --> 00:22:50.490
but let's say
semi-trusted, I guess.

00:22:50.490 --> 00:22:53.320
And that's because
maybe that organization

00:22:53.320 --> 00:22:57.340
has some sort of visibility
into the process and standards

00:22:57.340 --> 00:23:01.860
and knowledge of the software
that's going on those notes.

00:23:01.860 --> 00:23:05.240
You're not just letting a
regular user from the internet

00:23:05.240 --> 00:23:09.080
throw code up and start running
in one of your namespaces.

00:23:09.080 --> 00:23:12.170
And often, this paradigm
can parallel a lot

00:23:12.170 --> 00:23:15.050
with what we see in kind
of traditional IT, right?

00:23:15.050 --> 00:23:18.610
Where Kubernetes, you can
have almost a namespace admin,

00:23:18.610 --> 00:23:22.340
that almost can kind of be
the centralized IT function

00:23:22.340 --> 00:23:25.880
or how you're gatewaying
in or out of workloads.

00:23:25.880 --> 00:23:28.130
And the far other
extreme would almost--

00:23:28.130 --> 00:23:30.500
would be more of a
platform as a service

00:23:30.500 --> 00:23:32.600
or a Kubernetes as a service.

00:23:32.600 --> 00:23:37.910
You have completely untrusted
users running untrusted code,

00:23:37.910 --> 00:23:40.760
and they're having direct access
straight into the Kubernetes

00:23:40.760 --> 00:23:42.680
API and control plane.

00:23:42.680 --> 00:23:46.130
You might need to do things
like sandbox those workloads.

00:23:46.130 --> 00:23:49.190
You might need to isolate
them on physical VMs

00:23:49.190 --> 00:23:52.190
or isolate them by
various node pools.

00:23:52.190 --> 00:23:53.900
You could even need
to look at something

00:23:53.900 --> 00:23:56.600
like G Advisor or
Kata Containers

00:23:56.600 --> 00:23:59.660
to get that level of isolation.

00:23:59.660 --> 00:24:02.900
Our platform is more of
a software as a service,

00:24:02.900 --> 00:24:05.780
so it's really kind
of an in between.

00:24:05.780 --> 00:24:08.900
The user interfaces
directly with our core APIs.

00:24:08.900 --> 00:24:11.090
We don't actually
give direct access

00:24:11.090 --> 00:24:13.540
to any of our Kubernetes
control planes.

00:24:13.540 --> 00:24:16.790
And so those core APIs become
that funnel or that proxy

00:24:16.790 --> 00:24:19.960
where all the traffic and the
interaction with the clusters

00:24:19.960 --> 00:24:21.290
is coming.

00:24:21.290 --> 00:24:27.290
And so we need to allow a
range of models to be executed.

00:24:27.290 --> 00:24:31.650
Those are all Python code
on our API currently.

00:24:31.650 --> 00:24:33.950
And so we want to isolate
each of those workers

00:24:33.950 --> 00:24:37.640
by their own namespace, so
we can execute them and then

00:24:37.640 --> 00:24:41.680
have them only
interact with our APIs.

00:24:41.680 --> 00:24:44.320
So where does Istio come in?

00:24:44.320 --> 00:24:48.990
As those core APIs become
that central proxy,

00:24:48.990 --> 00:24:51.070
we need to get as much
visibility into that.

00:24:51.070 --> 00:24:53.470
That affects how the
cluster's scaling.

00:24:53.470 --> 00:24:58.300
That affects how
much money we spend--

00:24:58.300 --> 00:25:00.410
things like that, that
we really care about.

00:25:00.410 --> 00:25:03.310
So visibility becomes
absolutely critical.

00:25:03.310 --> 00:25:06.160
And with distributed
microservices,

00:25:06.160 --> 00:25:08.930
one of the biggest challenges
is around the network.

00:25:08.930 --> 00:25:11.380
You've broken applications
out, and they can run

00:25:11.380 --> 00:25:13.220
in many different locations.

00:25:13.220 --> 00:25:16.900
So getting visibility into where
different services are running

00:25:16.900 --> 00:25:19.690
in that inter-service
communication

00:25:19.690 --> 00:25:24.680
is absolutely important
to get metrics in control.

00:25:24.680 --> 00:25:27.040
And I would imagine
probably a lot of people--

00:25:27.040 --> 00:25:30.730
and there's references in
some of the talks already--

00:25:30.730 --> 00:25:32.110
are familiar with Istio.

00:25:32.110 --> 00:25:33.550
It's an open source platform.

00:25:33.550 --> 00:25:36.010
Google and a number of
others have contributed.

00:25:36.010 --> 00:25:41.170
It gives us load-balancing
for HTTP and GRPC traffic.

00:25:41.170 --> 00:25:43.120
You get fine-grained
control of traffic

00:25:43.120 --> 00:25:46.720
and routing, retries,
things like failover,

00:25:46.720 --> 00:25:52.240
fault injection, pluggable
policies, automatic metrics,

00:25:52.240 --> 00:25:55.440
logs, traces, things like that.

00:25:55.440 --> 00:25:59.320
In a way, it kind of
sounds a bit like magic.

00:25:59.320 --> 00:26:04.270
But in a practical way, it's
actually quite a bit simpler.

00:26:04.270 --> 00:26:07.820
This is a diagram of the
actual components there.

00:26:07.820 --> 00:26:12.160
And really, one of the keys
of Istio is the Envoy Proxy.

00:26:12.160 --> 00:26:14.440
Instead of adding
client libraries

00:26:14.440 --> 00:26:17.410
and adding a client library
for every single service,

00:26:17.410 --> 00:26:20.050
if I have a node app, I need
instrumentation libraries.

00:26:20.050 --> 00:26:22.780
If I have Python, I need that.

00:26:22.780 --> 00:26:25.140
If you even have something--
like we have Rust--

00:26:25.140 --> 00:26:29.410
maybe you need instrumentation
libraries for all of those.

00:26:29.410 --> 00:26:31.720
That Envoy Proxy,
instead, gets deployed

00:26:31.720 --> 00:26:33.250
into your existing code.

00:26:33.250 --> 00:26:35.480
This could be a Python server.

00:26:35.480 --> 00:26:37.100
It could be a Tomcat server.

00:26:37.100 --> 00:26:38.900
It could be anything like that.

00:26:38.900 --> 00:26:42.850
And so all the traffic now
goes through that proxy.

00:26:42.850 --> 00:26:47.320
And the proxy
automatically talks back

00:26:47.320 --> 00:26:49.390
to the pilot component,
and the pilot

00:26:49.390 --> 00:26:52.420
tells it where every
single service is,

00:26:52.420 --> 00:26:57.820
where load-balancing is, if
there's any routing rules that

00:26:57.820 --> 00:26:58.900
are in effect.

00:26:58.900 --> 00:27:01.870
And then the mixer is
where all of the telemetry

00:27:01.870 --> 00:27:05.260
and information
starts to come in.

00:27:05.260 --> 00:27:08.170
There's a pluggable log
layer where all your logs

00:27:08.170 --> 00:27:09.400
can get sucked up.

00:27:09.400 --> 00:27:11.980
It could go into
something like Prometheus.

00:27:11.980 --> 00:27:15.196
In our case, we'll use
things more like Stackdriver.

00:27:15.196 --> 00:27:16.570
And then finally,
you have things

00:27:16.570 --> 00:27:21.410
like MTLS for
service-to-service encryption.

00:27:21.410 --> 00:27:23.980
So if you're building
a SaaS platform,

00:27:23.980 --> 00:27:26.830
there's some additional
benefits to consider.

00:27:26.830 --> 00:27:31.120
Maybe you might be thinking
about ingress or egress.

00:27:31.120 --> 00:27:33.670
You might say, I'll
allow everything

00:27:33.670 --> 00:27:37.840
at star.googleapis.com to
flow out into the world,

00:27:37.840 --> 00:27:40.600
but I'm not going to actually
allow other sets of traffic.

00:27:40.600 --> 00:27:43.000
You can put that
type of rule there.

00:27:43.000 --> 00:27:47.920
Maybe you're getting a lot
of 500s, and for every 500,

00:27:47.920 --> 00:27:49.930
you think it might
be a network blip.

00:27:49.930 --> 00:27:51.490
You might want to do automatic--

00:27:51.490 --> 00:27:54.220
I might want to retry it
three times before I actually

00:27:54.220 --> 00:27:56.840
give it-- return back a 500.

00:27:56.840 --> 00:27:58.630
Maybe you'd like to
do rate limiting.

00:27:58.630 --> 00:28:02.320
Your auth server might be
hit five times a second.

00:28:02.320 --> 00:28:03.610
That might be great.

00:28:03.610 --> 00:28:07.590
But if it gets hit 50,000
times a second from one pod,

00:28:07.590 --> 00:28:12.130
well, I usually would
call that a DDoS.

00:28:12.130 --> 00:28:17.390
So that type of thing is what
we try and get out of Istio.

00:28:20.270 --> 00:28:24.730
So here's a diagram of what
things look like afterwards.

00:28:24.730 --> 00:28:27.020
And overall, it's actually
not that different.

00:28:27.020 --> 00:28:28.820
That's actually really awesome.

00:28:28.820 --> 00:28:30.590
You're not rewriting
that code just

00:28:30.590 --> 00:28:33.410
to get that network
reliability and that visibility

00:28:33.410 --> 00:28:35.120
between each of your services.

00:28:35.120 --> 00:28:37.640
We're now injecting the
side car in with each

00:28:37.640 --> 00:28:38.570
of those workloads.

00:28:41.390 --> 00:28:44.300
Envoy reports all traffic
telemetry straight up

00:28:44.300 --> 00:28:46.910
into the Istio mixer
and provides visibility

00:28:46.910 --> 00:28:47.900
into each service--

00:28:47.900 --> 00:28:52.140
who's calling the service, the
service-to-service automations.

00:28:52.140 --> 00:28:55.730
Overall, we gain amazing
insights into our core APIs

00:28:55.730 --> 00:28:57.710
that we actually
did not have before.

00:29:00.250 --> 00:29:04.990
So with that, we
would like to do

00:29:04.990 --> 00:29:11.480
a quick demonstration of some
of the multi-tenancy workloads.

00:29:11.480 --> 00:29:14.609
And we will do a quick switch.

00:29:14.609 --> 00:29:15.400
SAM SKILLMAN: Cool.

