Earth every five days or so.

00:32:11.390 --> 00:32:14.690
I create a composite
from that and then

00:32:14.690 --> 00:32:19.380
run this large neural
net transfer of one

00:32:19.380 --> 00:32:22.140
style to another.

00:32:22.140 --> 00:32:26.880
The details would take, again,
much longer than we have left.

00:32:26.880 --> 00:32:28.970
So what I've done is I've
already actually started

00:32:28.970 --> 00:32:31.470
running some of those while I
was sitting in the chair right

00:32:31.470 --> 00:32:34.000
here.

00:32:34.000 --> 00:32:38.070
But what you can see
happening on the backend

00:32:38.070 --> 00:32:40.635
is that this has taken a--

00:32:44.610 --> 00:32:47.610
taken our kind of
internal namespace

00:32:47.610 --> 00:32:49.980
for each individual
user and started

00:32:49.980 --> 00:32:52.840
scaling up the workload here.

00:32:52.840 --> 00:32:56.580
So if I get a new fresh look
on here, this is climbing up.

00:32:56.580 --> 00:33:01.530
This is 200 cores at the moment.

00:33:01.530 --> 00:33:05.500
So this started at 107
about six minutes ago.

00:33:05.500 --> 00:33:09.450
And as the system responds
to increased load, to more

00:33:09.450 --> 00:33:11.730
tasks running,
needing to be run,

00:33:11.730 --> 00:33:14.640
it continues to scale that
up in a way that doesn't just

00:33:14.640 --> 00:33:17.160
send a DDoS attack to
our internal services.

00:33:17.160 --> 00:33:20.660
So it kind of gradually
increases load over time.

00:33:20.660 --> 00:33:24.370
We'll come back to
this in a second.

00:33:24.370 --> 00:33:26.630
Let's see.

00:33:26.630 --> 00:33:30.350
So a lot of these
ones were quick ones,

00:33:30.350 --> 00:33:37.220
but then what this
has done is then

00:33:37.220 --> 00:33:41.090
taken that Sentinel-2
